    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female with progressive gait difficulty is evaluated. The clinician performs the **Tinetti Performance Oriented Mobility Assessment (POMA)**, which evaluates both static balance (9 items) and gait characteristics (8 items). The patient scores **17 out of a maximum 28 points**. What is the significance of this score, and how is it structured?', '["A low Tinetti score is a diagnostic criteria for sarcopenia and requires immediate androgen replacement therapy", "The POMA is used exclusively to evaluate patients after a hip fracture and has no role in community-dwelling fall risk screening", "A score of <19 indicates a high risk of falls (scores 19-24 indicate moderate risk); the test evaluates specific gait parameters (like step length and symmetry) and balance parameters (like nudge tolerance)", "A score of 17/28 is a normal finding; the POMA is primarily used to screen for peripheral neuropathy", "The Tinetti POMA is a cognitive screening tool; a score of 17 indicates moderate-to-severe dementia"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **Tinetti Performance Oriented Mobility Assessment (POMA)** is a task-oriented test that measures an older adult''s gait and balance abilities. It has two subscales: **Balance** (max 16 points, evaluating sitting balance, rising, standing balance, turning, and tolerance to a gentle sternal nudge) and **Gait** (max 12 points, evaluating step initiation, step height, step length, step symmetry, path deviation, and trunk stability). The maximum total score is 28. Scoring interpretation: **<19 = High fall risk**; **19–24 = Moderate fall risk**; **>24 = Low fall risk**. With a score of 17, this patient is at high risk of falls and requires targeted physical therapy and multifactorial fall interventions. (Ref: Tinetti ME, JAMA 1986; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 24)'),
