    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old male with progressive gait difficulty is evaluated. The clinician performs the **Tinetti Performance Oriented Mobility Assessment (POMA)**, which evaluates both static balance (9 items) and gait characteristics (8 items). The patient scores **15 out of a maximum 28 points**. What is the significance of this score, and how is it structured?', '["A score of <19 indicates a high risk of falls (scores 19-24 indicate moderate risk); the test evaluates specific gait parameters (like step length and symmetry) and balance parameters (like nudge tolerance)", "A low Tinetti score is a diagnostic criteria for sarcopenia and requires immediate androgen replacement therapy", "The Tinetti POMA is a cognitive screening tool; a score of 15 indicates moderate-to-severe dementia", "The POMA is used exclusively to evaluate patients after a hip fracture and has no role in community-dwelling fall risk screening", "A score of 15/28 is a normal finding; the POMA is primarily used to screen for peripheral neuropathy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **Tinetti Performance Oriented Mobility Assessment (POMA)** is a task-oriented test that measures an older adult''s gait and balance abilities. It has two subscales: **Balance** (max 16 points, evaluating sitting balance, rising, standing balance, turning, and tolerance to a gentle sternal nudge) and **Gait** (max 12 points, evaluating step initiation, step height, step length, step symmetry, path deviation, and trunk stability). The maximum total score is 28. Scoring interpretation: **<19 = High fall risk**; **19–24 = Moderate fall risk**; **>24 = Low fall risk**. With a score of 15, this patient is at high risk of falls and requires targeted physical therapy and multifactorial fall interventions. (Ref: Tinetti ME, JAMA 1986; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 24)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old female is evaluated for balance deficits during a wellness visit. The clinician performs the **Functional Reach Test** by instructing the patient to stand adjacent to a wall, make a fist, extend her arm, and reach as far forward as possible without taking a step or losing her balance. The patient achieves a reach of **5 inches**. What is the clinical significance of this result?', '["The Functional Reach Test measures cardiac output and predicts myocardial infarction in older adults", "The test is designed only to measure shoulder joint range of motion and has no correlation with postural stability or falls", "A reach of 5 inches indicates severe spinal stenosis; the patient should be restricted from all physical activity", "A reach of 5 inches is exceptional, demonstrating excellent postural control and zero risk of falls", "A functional reach of <10 inches (especially <6-7 inches) indicates a significantly restricted margin of stability and a high risk of falls; refer the patient for balance training"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Functional Reach Test** is a quick, single-item clinical tool used to measure a patient''s **margin of stability** and dynamic balance. It measures the maximum distance a patient can reach forward beyond arm''s length while maintaining a fixed base of support. Scoring and fall risk: **Reach <10 inches (25 cm) indicates increased fall risk**, with a **reach of <6-7 inches indicating a very high fall risk** (associated with a 4-fold increase in fall risk). Since this patient reached 5 inches, they have impaired dynamic balance and require further evaluation and balance training. (Ref: Duncan et al., J Gerontol 1990; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 24)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old woman is evaluated for balance deficits during a wellness visit. The clinician performs the **Functional Reach Test** by instructing the patient to stand adjacent to a wall, make a fist, extend her arm, and reach as far forward as possible without taking a step or losing her balance. The patient achieves a reach of **9 inches**. What is the clinical significance of this result?', '["The Functional Reach Test measures cardiac output and predicts myocardial infarction in older adults", "A reach of 9 inches is exceptional, demonstrating excellent postural control and zero risk of falls", "A functional reach of <10 inches (especially <6-7 inches) indicates a significantly restricted margin of stability and a high risk of falls; refer the patient for balance training", "The test is designed only to measure shoulder joint range of motion and has no correlation with postural stability or falls", "A reach of 9 inches indicates severe spinal stenosis; the patient should be restricted from all physical activity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **Functional Reach Test** is a quick, single-item clinical tool used to measure a patient''s **margin of stability** and dynamic balance. It measures the maximum distance a patient can reach forward beyond arm''s length while maintaining a fixed base of support. Scoring and fall risk: **Reach <10 inches (25 cm) indicates increased fall risk**, with a **reach of <6-7 inches indicating a very high fall risk** (associated with a 4-fold increase in fall risk). Since this patient reached 9 inches, they have impaired dynamic balance and require further evaluation and balance training. (Ref: Duncan et al., J Gerontol 1990; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 24)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old woman is evaluated for balance deficits during a wellness visit. The clinician performs the **Functional Reach Test** by instructing the patient to stand adjacent to a wall, make a fist, extend her arm, and reach as far forward as possible without taking a step or losing her balance. The patient achieves a reach of **4 inches**. What is the clinical significance of this result?', '["A functional reach of <10 inches (especially <6-7 inches) indicates a significantly restricted margin of stability and a high risk of falls; refer the patient for balance training", "The Functional Reach Test measures cardiac output and predicts myocardial infarction in older adults", "The test is designed only to measure shoulder joint range of motion and has no correlation with postural stability or falls", "A reach of 4 inches indicates severe spinal stenosis; the patient should be restricted from all physical activity", "A reach of 4 inches is exceptional, demonstrating excellent postural control and zero risk of falls"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **Functional Reach Test** is a quick, single-item clinical tool used to measure a patient''s **margin of stability** and dynamic balance. It measures the maximum distance a patient can reach forward beyond arm''s length while maintaining a fixed base of support. Scoring and fall risk: **Reach <10 inches (25 cm) indicates increased fall risk**, with a **reach of <6-7 inches indicating a very high fall risk** (associated with a 4-fold increase in fall risk). Since this patient reached 4 inches, they have impaired dynamic balance and require further evaluation and balance training. (Ref: Duncan et al., J Gerontol 1990; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 24)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old female is evaluated for balance deficits during a wellness visit. The clinician performs the **Functional Reach Test** by instructing the patient to stand adjacent to a wall, make a fist, extend her arm, and reach as far forward as possible without taking a step or losing her balance. The patient achieves a reach of **5 inches**. What is the clinical significance of this result?', '["A functional reach of <10 inches (especially <6-7 inches) indicates a significantly restricted margin of stability and a high risk of falls; refer the patient for balance training", "A reach of 5 inches indicates severe spinal stenosis; the patient should be restricted from all physical activity", "A reach of 5 inches is exceptional, demonstrating excellent postural control and zero risk of falls", "The Functional Reach Test measures cardiac output and predicts myocardial infarction in older adults", "The test is designed only to measure shoulder joint range of motion and has no correlation with postural stability or falls"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **Functional Reach Test** is a quick, single-item clinical tool used to measure a patient''s **margin of stability** and dynamic balance. It measures the maximum distance a patient can reach forward beyond arm''s length while maintaining a fixed base of support. Scoring and fall risk: **Reach <10 inches (25 cm) indicates increased fall risk**, with a **reach of <6-7 inches indicating a very high fall risk** (associated with a 4-fold increase in fall risk). Since this patient reached 5 inches, they have impaired dynamic balance and require further evaluation and balance training. (Ref: Duncan et al., J Gerontol 1990; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 24)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old man is evaluated for balance deficits during a wellness visit. The clinician performs the **Functional Reach Test** by instructing the patient to stand adjacent to a wall, make a fist, extend his arm, and reach as far forward as possible without taking a step or losing his balance. The patient achieves a reach of **4 inches**. What is the clinical significance of this result?', '["A reach of 4 inches indicates severe spinal stenosis; the patient should be restricted from all physical activity", "The test is designed only to measure shoulder joint range of motion and has no correlation with postural stability or falls", "A reach of 4 inches is exceptional, demonstrating excellent postural control and zero risk of falls", "A functional reach of <10 inches (especially <6-7 inches) indicates a significantly restricted margin of stability and a high risk of falls; refer the patient for balance training", "The Functional Reach Test measures cardiac output and predicts myocardial infarction in older adults"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **Functional Reach Test** is a quick, single-item clinical tool used to measure a patient''s **margin of stability** and dynamic balance. It measures the maximum distance a patient can reach forward beyond arm''s length while maintaining a fixed base of support. Scoring and fall risk: **Reach <10 inches (25 cm) indicates increased fall risk**, with a **reach of <6-7 inches indicating a very high fall risk** (associated with a 4-fold increase in fall risk). Since this patient reached 4 inches, they have impaired dynamic balance and require further evaluation and balance training. (Ref: Duncan et al., J Gerontol 1990; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 24)'),
