    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old woman is evaluated for balance deficits during a wellness visit. The clinician performs the **Functional Reach Test** by instructing the patient to stand adjacent to a wall, make a fist, extend her arm, and reach as far forward as possible without taking a step or losing her balance. The patient achieves a reach of **6 inches**. What is the clinical significance of this result?', '["A reach of 6 inches is exceptional, demonstrating excellent postural control and zero risk of falls", "The test is designed only to measure shoulder joint range of motion and has no correlation with postural stability or falls", "A functional reach of <10 inches (especially <6-7 inches) indicates a significantly restricted margin of stability and a high risk of falls; refer the patient for balance training", "The Functional Reach Test measures cardiac output and predicts myocardial infarction in older adults", "A reach of 6 inches indicates severe spinal stenosis; the patient should be restricted from all physical activity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **Functional Reach Test** is a quick, single-item clinical tool used to measure a patient''s **margin of stability** and dynamic balance. It measures the maximum distance a patient can reach forward beyond arm''s length while maintaining a fixed base of support. Scoring and fall risk: **Reach <10 inches (25 cm) indicates increased fall risk**, with a **reach of <6-7 inches indicating a very high fall risk** (associated with a 4-fold increase in fall risk). Since this patient reached 6 inches, they have impaired dynamic balance and require further evaluation and balance training. (Ref: Duncan et al., J Gerontol 1990; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 24)'),
