    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old woman is evaluated for balance deficits during a wellness visit. The clinician performs the **Functional Reach Test** by instructing the patient to stand adjacent to a wall, make a fist, extend her arm, and reach as far forward as possible without taking a step or losing her balance. The patient achieves a reach of **7 inches**. What is the clinical significance of this result?', '["A reach of 7 inches is exceptional, demonstrating excellent postural control and zero risk of falls", "The Functional Reach Test measures cardiac output and predicts myocardial infarction in older adults", "A reach of 7 inches indicates severe spinal stenosis; the patient should be restricted from all physical activity", "A functional reach of <10 inches (especially <6-7 inches) indicates a significantly restricted margin of stability and a high risk of falls; refer the patient for balance training", "The test is designed only to measure shoulder joint range of motion and has no correlation with postural stability or falls"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **Functional Reach Test** is a quick, single-item clinical tool used to measure a patient''s **margin of stability** and dynamic balance. It measures the maximum distance a patient can reach forward beyond arm''s length while maintaining a fixed base of support. Scoring and fall risk: **Reach <10 inches (25 cm) indicates increased fall risk**, with a **reach of <6-7 inches indicating a very high fall risk** (associated with a 4-fold increase in fall risk). Since this patient reached 7 inches, they have impaired dynamic balance and require further evaluation and balance training. (Ref: Duncan et al., J Gerontol 1990; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 24)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old woman is evaluated for balance deficits during a wellness visit. The clinician performs the **Functional Reach Test** by instructing the patient to stand adjacent to a wall, make a fist, extend her arm, and reach as far forward as possible without taking a step or losing her balance. The patient achieves a reach of **4 inches**. What is the clinical significance of this result?', '["The Functional Reach Test measures cardiac output and predicts myocardial infarction in older adults", "A reach of 4 inches indicates severe spinal stenosis; the patient should be restricted from all physical activity", "A reach of 4 inches is exceptional, demonstrating excellent postural control and zero risk of falls", "The test is designed only to measure shoulder joint range of motion and has no correlation with postural stability or falls", "A functional reach of <10 inches (especially <6-7 inches) indicates a significantly restricted margin of stability and a high risk of falls; refer the patient for balance training"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Functional Reach Test** is a quick, single-item clinical tool used to measure a patient''s **margin of stability** and dynamic balance. It measures the maximum distance a patient can reach forward beyond arm''s length while maintaining a fixed base of support. Scoring and fall risk: **Reach <10 inches (25 cm) indicates increased fall risk**, with a **reach of <6-7 inches indicating a very high fall risk** (associated with a 4-fold increase in fall risk). Since this patient reached 4 inches, they have impaired dynamic balance and require further evaluation and balance training. (Ref: Duncan et al., J Gerontol 1990; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 24)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male is evaluated for frailty using the **Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB)**. The assessment includes three components: a hierarchical balance test (side-by-side, semi-tandem, and full tandem stand), a 4-meter gait speed test, and a five-times chair stand test. The patient receives a total score of **6 out of 12**. Which of the following is the most accurate clinical interpretation of this score?', '["The chair stand component of the SPPB is used to diagnose severe osteoarthritis of the hip joint", "The SPPB is a cognitive test that replaces the MoCA in patients with severe sensory impairments", "A score of 6/12 indicates robust physical performance; the patient has a very low risk of developing functional dependence", "A score of \u22648 (out of 12) indicates significant physical impairment and is a strong predictor of future functional disability, hospitalization, institutionalization, and all-cause mortality; initiate physical therapy", "The SPPB is a diagnostic test for motor neuron disease; a score of 6 confirms amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB)** is a highly validated, objective tool used to measure lower extremity physical performance and identify frailty/sarcopenia. It consists of three parts (each scored 0–4): (1) **Balance testing** (holding side-by-side, semi-tandem, and full tandem positions for 10 seconds each); (2) **Gait speed** (walking 4 meters at normal pace); (3) **Five-times chair stand** (time taken to stand up and sit down 5 times from a chair without using arms). The total score is out of 12. Scoring interpretation: **≤8 indicates significant physical impairment** and is a powerful, independent predictor of future mobility disability, hospitalization, nursing home admission, and death. A score of 6/12 warrants physical therapy referral. (Ref: Guralnik et al., J Gerontol 1994; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 26)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old female is evaluated for frailty using the **Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB)**. The assessment includes three components: a hierarchical balance test (side-by-side, semi-tandem, and full tandem stand), a 4-meter gait speed test, and a five-times chair stand test. The patient receives a total score of **3 out of 12**. Which of the following is the most accurate clinical interpretation of this score?', '["The SPPB is a cognitive test that replaces the MoCA in patients with severe sensory impairments", "The SPPB is a diagnostic test for motor neuron disease; a score of 3 confirms amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)", "A score of 3/12 indicates robust physical performance; the patient has a very low risk of developing functional dependence", "The chair stand component of the SPPB is used to diagnose severe osteoarthritis of the hip joint", "A score of \u22648 (out of 12) indicates significant physical impairment and is a strong predictor of future functional disability, hospitalization, institutionalization, and all-cause mortality; initiate physical therapy"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB)** is a highly validated, objective tool used to measure lower extremity physical performance and identify frailty/sarcopenia. It consists of three parts (each scored 0–4): (1) **Balance testing** (holding side-by-side, semi-tandem, and full tandem positions for 10 seconds each); (2) **Gait speed** (walking 4 meters at normal pace); (3) **Five-times chair stand** (time taken to stand up and sit down 5 times from a chair without using arms). The total score is out of 12. Scoring interpretation: **≤8 indicates significant physical impairment** and is a powerful, independent predictor of future mobility disability, hospitalization, nursing home admission, and death. A score of 3/12 warrants physical therapy referral. (Ref: Guralnik et al., J Gerontol 1994; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 26)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old woman is evaluated for frailty using the **Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB)**. The assessment includes three components: a hierarchical balance test (side-by-side, semi-tandem, and full tandem stand), a 4-meter gait speed test, and a five-times chair stand test. The patient receives a total score of **4 out of 12**. Which of the following is the most accurate clinical interpretation of this score?', '["The SPPB is a cognitive test that replaces the MoCA in patients with severe sensory impairments", "A score of \u22648 (out of 12) indicates significant physical impairment and is a strong predictor of future functional disability, hospitalization, institutionalization, and all-cause mortality; initiate physical therapy", "The SPPB is a diagnostic test for motor neuron disease; a score of 4 confirms amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)", "The chair stand component of the SPPB is used to diagnose severe osteoarthritis of the hip joint", "A score of 4/12 indicates robust physical performance; the patient has a very low risk of developing functional dependence"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB)** is a highly validated, objective tool used to measure lower extremity physical performance and identify frailty/sarcopenia. It consists of three parts (each scored 0–4): (1) **Balance testing** (holding side-by-side, semi-tandem, and full tandem positions for 10 seconds each); (2) **Gait speed** (walking 4 meters at normal pace); (3) **Five-times chair stand** (time taken to stand up and sit down 5 times from a chair without using arms). The total score is out of 12. Scoring interpretation: **≤8 indicates significant physical impairment** and is a powerful, independent predictor of future mobility disability, hospitalization, nursing home admission, and death. A score of 4/12 warrants physical therapy referral. (Ref: Guralnik et al., J Gerontol 1994; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 26)'),
