    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old male is evaluated for frailty using the **Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB)**. The assessment includes three components: a hierarchical balance test (side-by-side, semi-tandem, and full tandem stand), a 4-meter gait speed test, and a five-times chair stand test. The patient receives a total score of **6 out of 12**. Which of the following is the most accurate clinical interpretation of this score?', '["The SPPB is a diagnostic test for motor neuron disease; a score of 6 confirms amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)", "A score of \u22648 (out of 12) indicates significant physical impairment and is a strong predictor of future functional disability, hospitalization, institutionalization, and all-cause mortality; initiate physical therapy", "A score of 6/12 indicates robust physical performance; the patient has a very low risk of developing functional dependence", "The SPPB is a cognitive test that replaces the MoCA in patients with severe sensory impairments", "The chair stand component of the SPPB is used to diagnose severe osteoarthritis of the hip joint"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB)** is a highly validated, objective tool used to measure lower extremity physical performance and identify frailty/sarcopenia. It consists of three parts (each scored 0–4): (1) **Balance testing** (holding side-by-side, semi-tandem, and full tandem positions for 10 seconds each); (2) **Gait speed** (walking 4 meters at normal pace); (3) **Five-times chair stand** (time taken to stand up and sit down 5 times from a chair without using arms). The total score is out of 12. Scoring interpretation: **≤8 indicates significant physical impairment** and is a powerful, independent predictor of future mobility disability, hospitalization, nursing home admission, and death. A score of 6/12 warrants physical therapy referral. (Ref: Guralnik et al., J Gerontol 1994; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 26)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment, a 74-year-old man is screened for cognitive impairment using the **Mini-Cog**. The patient successfully repeats three unrelated words (banana, sunrise, chair). After a brief interval during which he is asked to draw a clock face, put in the numbers, and set the hands to ''10 past 11'', he recalls only **1 of the 3 words**. The clock drawing is found to have all numbers in the correct positions, but the hands point to 10 and 11 instead of 2 and 11 (an abnormal clock). What is the patient''s Mini-Cog score, and what is the next step in management?', '["The Mini-Cog is a diagnostic tool for schizophrenia; a score of 1 indicates active psychosis requiring atypical antipsychotics", "The clock drawing component is highly subjective and should be ignored; because the patient recalled at least 1 word, the screen is negative", "Mini-Cog score is 3/5 (normal); reassure the patient and family that cognitive function is entirely intact", "Mini-Cog score is 1/5 (abnormal); this indicates a high likelihood of cognitive impairment and warrants a more detailed cognitive evaluation (e.g., MoCA) and diagnostic workup for dementia", "The abnormal clock indicates a focal cerebellar lesion; order an emergent head CT and neurology consultation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **Mini-Cog** is a rapid, 3-minute cognitive screening instrument scored out of 5. It consists of: (1) **3-word recall** (0–3 points); (2) **Clock Drawing Test (CDT)** (0 points if abnormal, 2 points if normal). A normal clock must have all numbers present in the correct order and the hands pointing to the correct time (typically 11:10 or 8:20). Scoring interpretation: **0–2 indicates high likelihood of cognitive impairment** (positive screen); **3–5 indicates low likelihood** (negative screen). This patient gets 1 point for the 1 recalled word and 0 points for the abnormal clock, resulting in a score of **1/5 (abnormal)**. This must trigger a comprehensive dementia evaluation, including a longer cognitive test (MoCA), lab work (TSH, B12, CMP, CBC), and neuroimaging (brain MRI). (Ref: Borson et al., Int J Geriatr Psychiatry 2000; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment, a 96-year-old woman is screened for cognitive impairment using the **Mini-Cog**. The patient successfully repeats three unrelated words (banana, sunrise, chair). After a brief interval during which she is asked to draw a clock face, put in the numbers, and set the hands to ''10 past 11'', she recalls only **1 of the 3 words**. The clock drawing is found to have all numbers in the correct positions, but the hands point to 10 and 11 instead of 2 and 11 (an abnormal clock). What is the patient''s Mini-Cog score, and what is the next step in management?', '["The Mini-Cog is a diagnostic tool for schizophrenia; a score of 1 indicates active psychosis requiring atypical antipsychotics", "The abnormal clock indicates a focal cerebellar lesion; order an emergent head CT and neurology consultation", "Mini-Cog score is 3/5 (normal); reassure the patient and family that cognitive function is entirely intact", "The clock drawing component is highly subjective and should be ignored; because the patient recalled at least 1 word, the screen is negative", "Mini-Cog score is 1/5 (abnormal); this indicates a high likelihood of cognitive impairment and warrants a more detailed cognitive evaluation (e.g., MoCA) and diagnostic workup for dementia"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Mini-Cog** is a rapid, 3-minute cognitive screening instrument scored out of 5. It consists of: (1) **3-word recall** (0–3 points); (2) **Clock Drawing Test (CDT)** (0 points if abnormal, 2 points if normal). A normal clock must have all numbers present in the correct order and the hands pointing to the correct time (typically 11:10 or 8:20). Scoring interpretation: **0–2 indicates high likelihood of cognitive impairment** (positive screen); **3–5 indicates low likelihood** (negative screen). This patient gets 1 point for the 1 recalled word and 0 points for the abnormal clock, resulting in a score of **1/5 (abnormal)**. This must trigger a comprehensive dementia evaluation, including a longer cognitive test (MoCA), lab work (TSH, B12, CMP, CBC), and neuroimaging (brain MRI). (Ref: Borson et al., Int J Geriatr Psychiatry 2000; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 72)'),
