    (v_topic_id, 'During a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment, a 95-year-old woman is screened for cognitive impairment using the **Mini-Cog**. The patient successfully repeats three unrelated words (banana, sunrise, chair). After a brief interval during which she is asked to draw a clock face, put in the numbers, and set the hands to ''10 past 11'', she recalls only **1 of the 3 words**. The clock drawing is found to have all numbers in the correct positions, but the hands point to 10 and 11 instead of 2 and 11 (an abnormal clock). What is the patient''s Mini-Cog score, and what is the next step in management?', '["Mini-Cog score is 1/5 (abnormal); this indicates a high likelihood of cognitive impairment and warrants a more detailed cognitive evaluation (e.g., MoCA) and diagnostic workup for dementia", "Mini-Cog score is 3/5 (normal); reassure the patient and family that cognitive function is entirely intact", "The clock drawing component is highly subjective and should be ignored; because the patient recalled at least 1 word, the screen is negative", "The abnormal clock indicates a focal cerebellar lesion; order an emergent head CT and neurology consultation", "The Mini-Cog is a diagnostic tool for schizophrenia; a score of 1 indicates active psychosis requiring atypical antipsychotics"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **Mini-Cog** is a rapid, 3-minute cognitive screening instrument scored out of 5. It consists of: (1) **3-word recall** (0–3 points); (2) **Clock Drawing Test (CDT)** (0 points if abnormal, 2 points if normal). A normal clock must have all numbers present in the correct order and the hands pointing to the correct time (typically 11:10 or 8:20). Scoring interpretation: **0–2 indicates high likelihood of cognitive impairment** (positive screen); **3–5 indicates low likelihood** (negative screen). This patient gets 1 point for the 1 recalled word and 0 points for the abnormal clock, resulting in a score of **1/5 (abnormal)**. This must trigger a comprehensive dementia evaluation, including a longer cognitive test (MoCA), lab work (TSH, B12, CMP, CBC), and neuroimaging (brain MRI). (Ref: Borson et al., Int J Geriatr Psychiatry 2000; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 72)'),
