a evaluation, including a longer cognitive test (MoCA), lab work (TSH, B12, CMP, CBC), and neuroimaging (brain MRI). (Ref: Borson et al., Int J Geriatr Psychiatry 2000; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment, a 75-year-old female is screened for cognitive impairment using the **Mini-Cog**. The patient successfully repeats three unrelated words (banana, sunrise, chair). After a brief interval during which she is asked to draw a clock face, put in the numbers, and set the hands to ''10 past 11'', she recalls only **1 of the 3 words**. The clock drawing is found to have all numbers in the correct positions, but the hands point to 10 and 11 instead of 2 and 11 (an abnormal clock). What is the patient''s Mini-Cog score, and what is the next step in management?', '["The clock drawing component is highly subjective and should be ignored; because the patient recalled at least 1 word, the screen is negative", "The Mini-Cog is a diagnostic tool for schizophrenia; a score of 1 indicates active psychosis requiring atypical antipsychotics", "Mini-Cog score is 3/5 (normal); reassure the patient and family that cognitive function is entirely intact", "Mini-Cog score is 1/5 (abnormal); this indicates a high likelihood of cognitive impairment and warrants a more detailed cognitive evaluation (e.g., MoCA) and diagnostic workup for dementia", "The abnormal clock indicates a focal cerebellar lesion; order an emergent head CT and neurology consultation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **Mini-Cog** is a rapid, 3-minute cognitive screening instrument scored out of 5. It consists of: (1) **3-word recall** (0–3 points); (2) **Clock Drawing Test (CDT)** (0 points if abnormal, 2 points if normal). A normal clock must have all numbers present in the correct order and the hands pointing to the correct time (typically 11:10 or 8:20). Scoring interpretation: **0–2 indicates high likelihood of cognitive impairment** (positive screen); **3–5 indicates low likelihood** (negative screen). This patient gets 1 point for the 1 recalled word and 0 points for the abnormal clock, resulting in a score of **1/5 (abnormal)**. This must trigger a comprehensive dementia evaluation, including a longer cognitive test (MoCA), lab work (TSH, B12, CMP, CBC), and neuroimaging (brain MRI). (Ref: Borson et al., Int J Geriatr Psychiatry 2000; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male is brought to the clinic by his family due to subtle changes in planning, multitasking, and driving. The patient scores **27/30** on the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE), which is in the ''normal'' range. However, the family insists that something is wrong. The clinician decides to administer the **Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA)**, and the patient scores **19/30**. Which of the following best explains why the MoCA detected a deficit that the MMSE missed?', '["The MoCA is a highly specific test for depression and somatic symptoms, whereas the MMSE only screens for language barriers", "The MMSE is highly sensitive to early changes in executive function, but the patient was likely malingering during the first test", "A score of 19/30 on the MoCA is a normal finding in older adults, while 27/30 on the MMSE indicates severe cortical atrophy", "The MoCA has superior sensitivity for detecting Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI) and early dementia because it includes more demanding tests of executive function (trail making, clock drawing, phonemic fluency) and abstract reasoning compared to the MMSE", "The MMSE is no longer used in clinical practice because it has been legally banned due to copyright infringement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE)** and the **Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA)** are both 30-point cognitive screens, but they have different clinical strengths. The **MMSE is heavily weighted toward memory and language** and has a significant ''ceiling effect''—meaning patients with high premorbid intelligence or subtle **executive dysfunction** (common in vascular cognitive impairment, frontotemporal dementia, or early Alzheimer''s/MCI) can score in the normal range (≥26/30). The **MoCA was specifically designed to detect Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI)**. It includes much more challenging tests of **executive functioning, visuospatial skills, and attention** (such as a 5-point executive/visuospatial section including a mini-trail making test, clock drawing, cube copy, and phonemic verbal fluency). MoCA sensitivity for MCI is ~90% (vs. ~18% for the MMSE). (Ref: Nasreddine et al., J Am Geriatr Soc 2005; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 72)'),
