    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old man is brought to the clinic by his family due to subtle changes in planning, multitasking, and driving. The patient scores **27/30** on the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE), which is in the ''normal'' range. However, the family insists that something is wrong. The clinician decides to administer the **Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA)**, and the patient scores **19/30**. Which of the following best explains why the MoCA detected a deficit that the MMSE missed?', '["A score of 19/30 on the MoCA is a normal finding in older adults, while 27/30 on the MMSE indicates severe cortical atrophy", "The MMSE is no longer used in clinical practice because it has been legally banned due to copyright infringement", "The MMSE is highly sensitive to early changes in executive function, but the patient was likely malingering during the first test", "The MoCA has superior sensitivity for detecting Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI) and early dementia because it includes more demanding tests of executive function (trail making, clock drawing, phonemic fluency) and abstract reasoning compared to the MMSE", "The MoCA is a highly specific test for depression and somatic symptoms, whereas the MMSE only screens for language barriers"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE)** and the **Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA)** are both 30-point cognitive screens, but they have different clinical strengths. The **MMSE is heavily weighted toward memory and language** and has a significant ''ceiling effect''—meaning patients with high premorbid intelligence or subtle **executive dysfunction** (common in vascular cognitive impairment, frontotemporal dementia, or early Alzheimer''s/MCI) can score in the normal range (≥26/30). The **MoCA was specifically designed to detect Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI)**. It includes much more challenging tests of **executive functioning, visuospatial skills, and attention** (such as a 5-point executive/visuospatial section including a mini-trail making test, clock drawing, cube copy, and phonemic verbal fluency). MoCA sensitivity for MCI is ~90% (vs. ~18% for the MMSE). (Ref: Nasreddine et al., J Am Geriatr Soc 2005; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old woman is brought to the clinic by her family due to subtle changes in planning, multitasking, and driving. The patient scores **27/30** on the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE), which is in the ''normal'' range. However, the family insists that something is wrong. The clinician decides to administer the **Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA)**, and the patient scores **19/30**. Which of the following best explains why the MoCA detected a deficit that the MMSE missed?', '["The MoCA has superior sensitivity for detecting Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI) and early dementia because it includes more demanding tests of executive function (trail making, clock drawing, phonemic fluency) and abstract reasoning compared to the MMSE", "The MMSE is no longer used in clinical practice because it has been legally banned due to copyright infringement", "A score of 19/30 on the MoCA is a normal finding in older adults, while 27/30 on the MMSE indicates severe cortical atrophy", "The MoCA is a highly specific test for depression and somatic symptoms, whereas the MMSE only screens for language barriers", "The MMSE is highly sensitive to early changes in executive function, but the patient was likely malingering during the first test"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE)** and the **Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA)** are both 30-point cognitive screens, but they have different clinical strengths. The **MMSE is heavily weighted toward memory and language** and has a significant ''ceiling effect''—meaning patients with high premorbid intelligence or subtle **executive dysfunction** (common in vascular cognitive impairment, frontotemporal dementia, or early Alzheimer''s/MCI) can score in the normal range (≥26/30). The **MoCA was specifically designed to detect Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI)**. It includes much more challenging tests of **executive functioning, visuospatial skills, and attention** (such as a 5-point executive/visuospatial section including a mini-trail making test, clock drawing, cube copy, and phonemic verbal fluency). MoCA sensitivity for MCI is ~90% (vs. ~18% for the MMSE). (Ref: Nasreddine et al., J Am Geriatr Soc 2005; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old male is evaluated for progressive social withdrawal, fatigue, and decreased appetite. To screen for depression, the clinician administers the **Geriatric Depression Scale (GDS-15)**. The patient scores **11 out of 15**. Which of the following statements regarding the GDS-15 is most accurate?', '["The GDS-15 is an obsolete tool that has been replaced entirely by the Hamilton Depression Rating Scale in older adults", "The GDS-15 is a diagnostic tool for bipolar disorder and requires immediate initiation of lithium therapy", "A score of \u22655 on the GDS-15 indicates a positive screen for depression; the tool is highly effective because it deliberately omits somatic symptoms (like fatigue, sleep changes, and weight loss) which frequently overlap with normal aging or physical illness in older adults", "A score of 11 is normal; the GDS-15 is only considered positive if the patient scores a perfect 15 out of 15", "Somatic symptoms are the most reliable indicators of depression in the elderly and are the primary focus of the GDS-15"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **Geriatric Depression Scale (GDS-15)** is a 15-item yes/no questionnaire used to screen for depression in older adults. A score of **≥5 indicates a positive screen for depression** (with ≥10 indicating moderate-to-severe depression). Because this patient scored 11/15, they screen positive. A critical design feature of the GDS is that it **omits somatic symptoms** (such as sleep disturbance, fatigue, changes in appetite, and weight loss). In older populations, these somatic symptoms are highly prevalent due to co-existing physical illnesses, medications, or normal aging, and their inclusion in standard depression screens (like the Beck Depression Inventory) leads to a high rate of false-positive results. By focusing on cognitive and affective symptoms (e.g., life satisfaction, boredom, helplessness, fear of the future), the GDS-15 provides a highly specific screening. (Ref: Yesavage et al., J Psychiatr Res 1983; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 75)'),
