    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old man is admitted to the medical ward for a urinary tract infection. On post-admission day 2, the patient becomes acutely confused, starts picking at imaginary objects in the air, and fluctuates between extreme agitation and lethargy. The nurse suspects delirium. To confirm this, the clinician utilizes the **Confusion Assessment Method (CAM)**. Which of the following describes the criteria required to diagnose delirium using the CAM?', '["Requires the presence of all five DSM-5 criteria for dementia including chronic, irreversible memory decline and cortical atrophy", "Requires a positive urine culture and an elevated white blood cell count; clinical features are secondary", "Requires only the presence of an altered level of consciousness, as acute fluctuations are normal in hospitalized older adults", "Requires the presence of both (1) Acute onset and fluctuating course, AND (2) Inattention; PLUS either (3) Disorganized thinking, OR (4) Altered level of consciousness", "Requires an abnormal brain MRI showing acute ischemic changes in the bilateral temporal lobes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **Confusion Assessment Method (CAM)** is the gold-standard bedside screening tool for **delirium**. To establish a diagnosis of delirium, the CAM requires the presence of: (1) **Acute onset and fluctuating course** (evidence of an acute change in mental status from baseline that fluctuates in severity); **AND** (2) **Inattention** (difficulty focusing, easily distracted); **PLUS** either (3) **Disorganized thinking** (rambling conversation, illogical flow) **OR** (4) **Altered level of consciousness** (ranging from hyperalert/agitated to lethargic/stuporous). Delirium is an acute medical emergency representing acute brain dysfunction, usually triggered by underlying medical illnesses (infections, drugs, dehydration) in vulnerable hosts. (Ref: Inouye et al., Ann Intern Med 1990; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 27)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old male is admitted to the medical ward for a urinary tract infection. On post-admission day 2, the patient becomes acutely confused, starts picking at imaginary objects in the air, and fluctuates between extreme agitation and lethargy. The nurse suspects delirium. To confirm this, the clinician utilizes the **Confusion Assessment Method (CAM)**. Which of the following describes the criteria required to diagnose delirium using the CAM?', '["Requires only the presence of an altered level of consciousness, as acute fluctuations are normal in hospitalized older adults", "Requires the presence of both (1) Acute onset and fluctuating course, AND (2) Inattention; PLUS either (3) Disorganized thinking, OR (4) Altered level of consciousness", "Requires the presence of all five DSM-5 criteria for dementia including chronic, irreversible memory decline and cortical atrophy", "Requires an abnormal brain MRI showing acute ischemic changes in the bilateral temporal lobes", "Requires a positive urine culture and an elevated white blood cell count; clinical features are secondary"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **Confusion Assessment Method (CAM)** is the gold-standard bedside screening tool for **delirium**. To establish a diagnosis of delirium, the CAM requires the presence of: (1) **Acute onset and fluctuating course** (evidence of an acute change in mental status from baseline that fluctuates in severity); **AND** (2) **Inattention** (difficulty focusing, easily distracted); **PLUS** either (3) **Disorganized thinking** (rambling conversation, illogical flow) **OR** (4) **Altered level of consciousness** (ranging from hyperalert/agitated to lethargic/stuporous). Delirium is an acute medical emergency representing acute brain dysfunction, usually triggered by underlying medical illnesses (infections, drugs, dehydration) in vulnerable hosts. (Ref: Inouye et al., Ann Intern Med 1990; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 27)'),
