    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old male is undergoing a wellness evaluation. The clinician administers the **Mini Nutritional Assessment Short-Form (MNA-SF)**, which covers food intake decline, weight loss, mobility, acute stress/disease, neuropsychological problems, and Body Mass Index (BMI). The patient receives a score of **10 out of 14**. What is the clinical significance of this score, and what is the next step in management?', '["The MNA-SF is a cognitive screening tool; a score of 10 indicates that the patient has advanced dementia", "A low MNA-SF score indicates that the patient should be placed on a strict low-calorie diet to prevent metabolic syndrome", "A score of 10/14 is optimal, indicating excellent nutritional status; no interventions or changes are needed", "The MNA-SF is a diagnostic tool for celiac disease; a low score requires an immediate referral for upper endoscopy and duodenal biopsy", "A score of \u226411 out of 14 indicates a risk of malnutrition (with \u22647 indicating malnutrition); this patient requires a detailed nutritional assessment, dietary review, and targeted nutritional interventions"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Mini Nutritional Assessment Short-Form (MNA-SF)** is a rapid, validated screening tool designed to identify nutritional issues in older adults. It is scored out of 14 points. Scoring thresholds: **12–14 points = Normal nutritional status**; **8–11 points = At risk of malnutrition**; **0–7 points = Malnourished**. Because this patient scored 10/14, they are ''at risk of malnutrition'' (or malnourished if ≤7). This positive screen must trigger: (1) A comprehensive dietary assessment (calorie counts, food preferences); (2) Evaluation of reversible causes (poor dentition, dysphagia, social isolation, depression, medications, financial limits); (3) Weight monitoring and consideration of nutritional supplements or referral to a dietitian. (Ref: Guigoz et al., J Nutr Health Aging 2006; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 29)'),
