    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old male is undergoing a wellness evaluation. The clinician administers the **Mini Nutritional Assessment Short-Form (MNA-SF)**, which covers food intake decline, weight loss, mobility, acute stress/disease, neuropsychological problems, and Body Mass Index (BMI). The patient receives a score of **6 out of 14**. What is the clinical significance of this score, and what is the next step in management?', '["A low MNA-SF score indicates that the patient should be placed on a strict low-calorie diet to prevent metabolic syndrome", "A score of 6/14 is optimal, indicating excellent nutritional status; no interventions or changes are needed", "The MNA-SF is a cognitive screening tool; a score of 6 indicates that the patient has advanced dementia", "The MNA-SF is a diagnostic tool for celiac disease; a low score requires an immediate referral for upper endoscopy and duodenal biopsy", "A score of \u226411 out of 14 indicates a risk of malnutrition (with \u22647 indicating malnutrition); this patient requires a detailed nutritional assessment, dietary review, and targeted nutritional interventions"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Mini Nutritional Assessment Short-Form (MNA-SF)** is a rapid, validated screening tool designed to identify nutritional issues in older adults. It is scored out of 14 points. Scoring thresholds: **12–14 points = Normal nutritional status**; **8–11 points = At risk of malnutrition**; **0–7 points = Malnourished**. Because this patient scored 6/14, they are ''at risk of malnutrition'' (or malnourished if ≤7). This positive screen must trigger: (1) A comprehensive dietary assessment (calorie counts, food preferences); (2) Evaluation of reversible causes (poor dentition, dysphagia, social isolation, depression, medications, financial limits); (3) Weight monitoring and consideration of nutritional supplements or referral to a dietitian. (Ref: Guigoz et al., J Nutr Health Aging 2006; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 29)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old male presents with progressive weight loss. The patient''s spouse notes that his clothes have become noticeably baggy. A review of clinic records confirms that the patient''s weight has dropped from 160 lbs to 142 lbs over the past 6 months (a **8% decline**), which was unintentional. He has no specific pain, fever, or changes in bowel habits. Which of the following is the standard definition of clinically significant weight loss in older adults, and what is the most appropriate initial diagnostic workup?', '["Clinically significant weight loss is defined as a drop of at least 25% of body weight in 3 months; the initial step is an immediate whole-body CT scan and PET scan to look for occult malignancy", "Unintentional weight loss of \u22655% of body weight in 6 months (or \u226510% in 1 year) is clinically significant; the initial workup includes a comprehensive history, physical exam, medication review, depression screening, and basic laboratory tests (CBC, CMP, TSH, B12, urinalysis, stool guaiac)", "Weight loss of \u22655% requires immediate placement of a percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) tube to prevent further decline", "The initial step is to discontinue all oral antihypertensive medications as they are the primary cause of muscle wasting", "Any weight loss in older adults is considered normal and benign; reassure the patient and recommend high-fat foods only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Clinically significant **unintentional weight loss** in older adults is defined as an involuntary weight loss of **≥5% of body weight in 6 months, or ≥10% in 1 year**. It is associated with increased morbidity and mortality. The initial diagnostic approach should be systematic and non-invasive, starting with a **detailed history, physical exam, review of medications (polypharmacy), depression screening (GDS), and baseline labs** (Complete Blood Count, Complete Metabolic Panel to check renal/hepatic function and albumin, Thyroid Stimulating Hormone to rule out hyperthyroidism, Vitamin B12, urinalysis, and fecal occult blood testing). Advanced imaging (CT/PET) is NOT recommended as an initial screen unless specific red-flag symptoms or focal physical findings suggest malignancy. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 29; Alibhai et al., Am J Med 2005)'),
