 to rule out hyperthyroidism, Vitamin B12, urinalysis, and fecal occult blood testing). Advanced imaging (CT/PET) is NOT recommended as an initial screen unless specific red-flag symptoms or focal physical findings suggest malignancy. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 29; Alibhai et al., Am J Med 2005)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old woman presents for a routine physical. The clinician performs a **Snellen chart** visual acuity test, and the patient''s vision is **20/60** in the right eye and **20/80** in the left eye with her current glasses. She reports difficulty reading labels and driving at night. Which of the following statements regarding vision impairment screening in the CGA is most accurate?', '["Vision impairment (acuity worse than 20/40) is a major risk factor for falls, hip fractures, depression, and cognitive decline; refer the patient to ophthalmology for a comprehensive exam to evaluate for cataracts, age-related macular degeneration (AMD), glaucoma, or refractive error", "The Snellen chart is a diagnostic test for glaucoma; a score of 20/80 confirms closed-angle glaucoma", "Vision of 20/80 is considered normal in this age group; reassure the patient that no further ophthalmological evaluations are necessary", "The patient should be immediately reported to the state licensing agency to have her driver''s license permanently revoked without further testing", "Prescribe low-dose atypical antipsychotics to manage the patient''s complaints of nighttime driving difficulty"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Vision impairment is highly prevalent in older adults and is a critical domain in the Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment (CGA). Visual acuity of **worse than 20/40 is abnormal**. Uncorrected vision loss is a powerful **independent risk factor for falls, hip fractures, social isolation, depression, and accelerated cognitive decline**. The Snellen chart is a screening tool, not a diagnostic one. A positive screen (acuity worse than 20/40, or new symptoms) must prompt a referral to an ophthalmologist or optometrist to identify and treat reversible or progressive conditions, including: (1) Refractive errors; (2) **Cataracts** (most common cause of reversible vision loss); (3) **Age-related Macular Degeneration (AMD)** (leading cause of irreversible blindness); (4) **Glaucoma**; (5) **Diabetic retinopathy**. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
